// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    The Courtroom Summation
// @category Oratory / Persuasion
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Simple_Country_Lawyer
// @domain   Narrative
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): the closing argument — an advocate's final address to the jury before they
// deliberate, marshalling the already-entered EVIDENCE (logos) and the emotional case (pathos) and
// asking, under the burden of proof, for a VERDICT. FRONTIER: the persuasion machinery turned on a
// VERDICT-GIVER. The sibling rhetoric tropes either contend for a crowd (the_debate's two speakers),
// rally a band by pathos alone (rousing_speech), or run the defensive move of withholding belief
// (rogue_juror's reasonable doubt). The Courtroom Summation is the AFFIRMATIVE advocate's turn: one
// speaker, addressing a sworn [+Jury] whose assent IS the outcome, recapitulating the record that was
// entered into evidence and exhorting them to the verdict it compels. The discriminator is the
// addressee and the ask: a [+Jury] [+Arbiter] is exhorted toward a verdict, not a crowd toward a side
// (debate) or a band toward a cause (rousing). It rides persuasion's logos + pathos + call-to-action
// in one set piece — the call's target is the arbiter, and its action is the verdict.

import "trl/modules/persuasion.trl"          // logos (Argues/Cites) + pathos + the call-to-action to a verdict

// Lateral thematic links (optional, machine-followable — for discovery / embeddings).
assoc courtroom_summation -> [
  persuasion   : "stages all three appeals of, aimed at a verdict-giver",
  rogue_juror  : "is the advocate's affirmative turn against the defensive doubt of",
  the_debate   : "is the one-sided, jury-bound cousin of (an arbiter, not a contested crowd)",
  rousing_speech : "marries the evidence-marshalling logos that rallying skips to its pathos"
]

imply CourtroomSummation -> [Pathos, Conviction, Truth_Claim, Narrative]

// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: an advocate with standing marshals the entered RECORD (Cites the support) and exhorts
// a sworn [+Jury] toward the VERDICT it compels. The DISCRIMINATOR rides the call's target= role — the
// addressee is an [+Arbiter] [+Jury], not a crowd or a band — and the action= is the verdict, so both
// bind. The payoff is persuasion's COMMITMENT, derived here as the jury committed to the verdict; the
// burden-of-proof gate (a single doubt unseating it) is rogue_juror's defensive mirror, not this rule.
rule Courtroom_Summation {
  when:
    char $advocate [+Speaker] [+Credible]
    char $jury [+Jury] [+Arbiter]
    evt  $marshalling [&Cites(agent=$advocate, support=$record)]
    evt  $summation [&Exhorts(agent=$advocate, target=$jury, action=$verdict)]
  then:
    $jury [+Committed(to=$verdict)]
    $jury -- conviction
}

// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: To Kill a Mockingbird — Atticus Finch's closing argument (film, 1962) ===
// Tom Robinson, a Black man in Depression-era Alabama, is on trial for a rape the physical record makes
// impossible: Mayella Ewell's bruises are on the RIGHT side of her face — the blow of a left-handed man
// — and Tom's left arm was crippled in a cotton-gin years ago. Atticus rises for the summation. He does
// not call a new witness; he MARSHALS the record already entered — the handedness, the maimed arm, the
// absence of a doctor — then turns the logos to pathos: a court is the one place a poor man and a rich
// man, an ignorant man and a learned one, are equal. He asks the twelve, in the name of God, to do
// their duty. The ask is theirs to answer; the verdict is the whole of it.

// ATTICUS FINCH — the advocate; a country lawyer the town credits even as it disagrees with him
char atticus [+Speaker] [+Credible]
atticus [+Principled]

// THE JURY — twelve men of Maycomb County, the sworn arbiters whose assent is the verdict
char the_jury [+Jury] [+Arbiter]
the_jury [+Sworn]

// THE TRIAL RECORD — the evidence already entered: handedness, the crippled arm, no doctor called
obj the_record [+Evidence]
the_record [+Truth_Claim]

// THE VERDICT — acquittal; the one thing the summation is for
concept acquittal [%Narrative]

// INT. MAYCOMB COUNTY COURTHOUSE - DAY
set the_courthouse [+Interior] [+Day]
the_courthouse [+Crowded] [+Oppressive]

scene the_closing_argument {
  beat 1 {
    atticus @ the_courthouse
    the_jury @ the_courthouse
    // Marshal the already-entered record — the left-handed blow, the maimed arm. (logos: Cites)
    evt the_marshalling [&Cites(agent=atticus, support=the_record)]
        dialog(inject=[speaker=atticus], inline="""
               speaker: "{speaker}"
               cue: "Atticus, in summation, walks the jury back through the evidence already before them"
               tone: measured, grave, unhurried
               text: "The state has not produced one iota of medical evidence that the crime Tom Robinson is charged with ever took place. This case is as simple as black and white."
               """)
  }
  beat 2 {
    // The call: addressed to the twelve by their office, asking the verdict the record compels.
    // The discriminator binds here — target=the_jury (an [+Arbiter] [+Jury]), action=acquittal.
    evt the_appeal [&Exhorts(agent=atticus, target=the_jury, action=acquittal)]
        dialog(inject=[speaker=atticus], inline="""
               speaker: "{speaker}"
               cue: "Atticus turns from the evidence to the jury itself, the pathos under the logos"
               tone: imploring, solemn
               text: "In the name of God, do your duty. In the name of God, believe Tom Robinson."
               """)
    // Courtroom_Summation fires here: the credited advocate has marshalled the entered record (Cites)
    // and exhorted the sworn [+Jury] [+Arbiter] toward the verdict (Exhorts, target=the_jury,
    // action=acquittal). The engine DERIVES the_jury [+Committed(to=acquittal)] -- conviction; it is
    // the rule's payoff, not hand-asserted. (History, and Maycomb's prejudice, are the burden the
    // record could not carry past — rogue_juror's defensive gate, not this affirmative turn.)
  }
}
